--TEST--
Core\Str::slice and ::replaceRange count grapheme clusters, so a window that substr would cut through a code point and mb_substr through a cluster is taken whole instead
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
One divergence, from two twins at once, and it is a unit rather than an arithmetic disagreement: `Core\Str::slice`'s `int $offset` and `?int $length` are counted in **grapheme clusters** (ADR 0009 § 2, `nvs_stdlib::granularity::DEFAULT`), where `substr` counts **bytes** and `mb_substr` counts **code points**. Inside ASCII with no carriage return all three units are the same unit, which is why the whole sign table agrees in `str-slice-and-replace-range-match-substr-and-substr_replace`; outside it they part three ways. **Against `substr`.** `"aé中👍"` is 4 to `Core\Str::length` and 10 to `strlen`, and `substr($s, 1, 1)` is the single byte `c3` — the lead of `é`'s two-byte sequence, and not a `string` Novis can hold at all, since every Novis `string` is well-formed UTF-8 (ADR 0009 § 3 makes `bytes as string` the checked conversion precisely so that a half-decoded fragment cannot arrive by accident). `Core\Str::slice($s, 1, 1)` is `é`. The same one-byte cut of `"a👍🏽b"` is `f0`. **Against `mb_substr`.** Counting code points does keep the answer well-formed, but it still splits a cluster: `"a👍🏽b"` is 4 to `mb_strlen` and 3 here, because the thumbs-up and its skin-tone modifier are one user-perceived character, and `mb_substr($s, 1, 1)` is the unmodified `👍` where `Core\Str::slice($s, 1, 1)` is both code points. `"cafe\u{0301}"` is 5 to `mb_strlen` and 4 here, and the difference is visible through `replaceRange`: `substr_replace($s, "<>", 3, 1)` is `caf<>` with `e`'s combining acute left behind and now rendering on the `>`, while `Core\Str::replaceRange($s, 3, 1, "<>")` removes the whole cluster. Neither PHP function is *wrong* about its own unit; what Novis refuses is having two of them, so the one member counts the unit a caller means by "character" and there is no second spelling to pick between (ADR 0063 R20). The cost is that a port measuring a byte budget — a column width, a protocol field — has to say so: `Core\Str::length($s as bytes)` is the byte count, and this file's hex rows are what make the three units visible at once. There is no PHP on either leg to run this against — the `php` on the Windows `PATH` has no `mbstring`, so `mb_substr` is not even callable here — which is the second reason the expectation is frozen rather than computed.
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Spec § 1's `slice` and `replaceRange` rows over subjects where the byte,
// the code point and the grapheme cluster are three different counts. The
// agreeing half — the whole offset/length sign table over an ASCII subject,
// where the three units coincide — is in
// `str-slice-and-replace-range-match-substr-and-substr_replace`.

final class Show {
    // The subject's own bytes. A case cannot echo a `bytes` (ADR 0009 § 3),
    // and a lone continuation byte is not a `string` at all, so hex is how
    // the unit `substr` counts in becomes visible without ever holding one.
    public static function bytes(string $text): string {
        return Core\Encoding::toHex($text as bytes);
    }
}

// Three subjects, one per way the units can part. `$mixed` has one code point
// per cluster and four different byte widths; `$tone` has a two-code-point
// cluster, which is where the code-point unit stops agreeing; `$combining`
// has a base and a mark, which is the same split written with the mark after
// an ASCII letter, so a byte-counted window lands *inside* what a reader sees
// as one character rather than merely beside it.
string $mixed = "aé中👍";
string $tone = "a👍🏽b";
string $combining = "cafe\u{0301}";

// The count each subject answers, beside the bytes it is made of. Ten bytes
// and four characters, ten and three, six and four — `strlen` reads the first
// number of each pair and `Core\Str::length` the second.
echo Core\Str::length($mixed), " ", Show::bytes($mixed), "\n";
echo Core\Str::length($tone), " ", Show::bytes($tone), "\n";
echo Core\Str::length($combining), " ", Show::bytes($combining), "\n";

// One unit at a time, rendered and in hex. Every cell is a whole cluster: the
// widths run 1, 2, 3 and 4 bytes down the first row and reach 8 on the
// second, which is the claim — the window is measured in what a reader counts
// and materialized in whatever bytes that takes. The fourth cell of the
// second row is past the end of a three-cluster subject, so it is empty,
// which is `slice`'s answer for an offset past the end rather than a throw.
array<int> $indices = [0, 1, 2, 3];
foreach ($indices as int $index) {
    echo "[", Core\Str::slice($mixed, $index, 1), "=", Show::bytes(Core\Str::slice($mixed, $index, 1)), "]";
}
echo "\n";
foreach ($indices as int $spot) {
    echo "[", Core\Str::slice($tone, $spot, 1), "=", Show::bytes(Core\Str::slice($tone, $spot, 1)), "]";
}
echo "\n";
// `$combining`'s fourth cluster is rendered as hex alone, not beside itself:
// a decomposed `e`+U+0301 and a precomposed U+00E9 look identical in a text
// editor and are different strings, so an `--EXPECT--` block holding the
// rendered form would pin whichever one the file happened to be saved in.
foreach ($indices as int $place) {
    echo "[", Show::bytes(Core\Str::slice($combining, $place, 1)), "]";
}
echo "\n";

// The invariance, counted across all three subjects rather than read off the
// rows above: a window one unit wide answers exactly one unit, at every
// offset that has one. Eleven of the twelve cells do, and the twelfth is the
// out-of-range offset named above — so a member that counted bytes would
// score twelve here while its rows printed fragments, and one that counted
// code points would score twelve too but split `$tone`'s cluster on the way.
array<string> $subjects = ["aé中👍", "a👍🏽b", "cafe\u{0301}"];
int $whole = 0;
int $cells = 0;
foreach ($subjects as string $subject) {
    foreach ($indices as int $at) {
        $cells = $cells + 1;
        if (Core\Str::length(Core\Str::slice($subject, $at, 1)) == 1) {
            $whole = $whole + 1;
        }
    }
}
echo $whole, " of ", $cells, "\n";

// The window identity, over the same subjects and every sign of the length:
// putting a window's own slice back into it reproduces the subject. This is
// what says the two members read *one* window — `nvs_stdlib::str`'s `window`
// helper, which owns the sign rule — rather than two that agree over ASCII
// and drift once a cluster is wider than a byte. Seventy-two of seventy-two.
int $held = 0;
int $windows = 0;
array<int> $lengths = [0, 1, 2, 4, -1, -4];
foreach ($subjects as string $text) {
    foreach ($indices as int $anchor) {
        foreach ($lengths as int $reach) {
            $windows = $windows + 1;
            if (Core\Str::replaceRange($text, $anchor, $reach, Core\Str::slice($text, $anchor, $reach)) == $text) {
                $held = $held + 1;
            }
        }
    }
}
echo $held, " of ", $windows, "\n";

// The substitutions the header names one by one. The middle cluster of
// `$tone` goes whole, both of its code points; two clusters of `$mixed` go
// together; and `$combining`'s accented `e` goes with its mark, where
// `substr_replace($s, "<>", 3, 1)` takes the `e` alone and leaves the acute
// behind to render on the `>`.
echo "[", Core\Str::replaceRange($tone, 1, 1, "<>"), "][", Core\Str::replaceRange($mixed, 1, 2, "<>"), "][", Core\Str::replaceRange($combining, 3, 1, "<>"), "]\n";

// A negative offset counts clusters back from the end, not bytes, which is
// the sign rule of the agreeing file read in this file's unit.
echo "[", Core\Str::slice($mixed, -2), "][", Core\Str::slice($tone, -1), "][", Show::bytes(Core\Str::slice($combining, -1)), "]\n";

// The two clusters this file exists for, in the bytes they actually occupy:
// three bytes for a base plus a combining mark, eight for an emoji plus its
// modifier. Both are one unit to every member above.
echo "[", Show::bytes(Core\Str::slice($combining, 3, 1)), "][", Show::bytes(Core\Str::slice($tone, 1, 1)), "]\n";
--EXPECT--
4 61c3a9e4b8adf09f918d
3 61f09f918df09f8fbd62
4 63616665cc81
[a=61][é=c3a9][中=e4b8ad][👍=f09f918d]
[a=61][👍🏽=f09f918df09f8fbd][b=62][=]
[63][61][66][65cc81]
11 of 12
72 of 72
[a<>b][a<>👍][caf<>]
[中👍][b][65cc81]
[65cc81][f09f918df09f8fbd]
